i_name":"Buttermilk Falls","subject":"Craig Wood","author":"Dave Woten","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/92","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/92"}},{"id":"1060","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"2511","description":"","caption":"Buttermilk Falls","poi_name":"Buttermilk Falls","subject":"Craig Wood","author":"Dave Woten","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/1060","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/1060"}},{"id":"888962","ext":"png","post_id":"891610","description":"Smooth run on the right line. It was a nice little ride with a zig zag route and caught the eddy on the far right at the end of the rapid.","caption":"Buttermilk Falls right line","poi_name":"Buttermilk Falls","subject":"Jonathan Janicki","author":"Jonathan Janicki","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/888962","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/888962"}},{"id":"6697","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"6508","description":"","caption":"Just about done","poi_name":"","subject":"Martin W.","author":"Sam B.","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/6697","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/6697"}},{"id":"6694","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"6509","description":"","caption":"Another rapid","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Sam B.","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/6694","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/6694"}},{"id":"6698","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"6510","description":"","caption":"Last bigger rapid","poi_name":"","subject":"Jen F.","author":"Sam B","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/6698","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/6698"}},{"id":"6692","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"6510","description":"","caption":"First bigger rapid after alternate put in","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Sam B.","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/6692","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/6692"}},{"id":"6693","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"6510","description":"","caption":"A look ahead at the next rapid","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Sam B.","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/6693","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/6693"}},{"id":"6695","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"6510","description":"","caption":"Bridge Rapid, Look Out For Wood in this area","poi_name":"","subject":"","author":"Sam B.","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/6695","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/6695"}},{"id":"888961","ext":"png","post_id":"891609","description":"Here's the finish of a beautiful pry stroke to position my boat just to the left of a rock.","caption":"Pry stroke ","poi_name":"","subject":"Jonathan Janicki","author":"Jonathan Janicki","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/888961","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/888961"}},{"id":"888959","ext":"png","post_id":"891607","description":"The character of Cowanshannock is very different above and below Buttermilk Falls. Simple ledges with big holes give way to very technical rock gardens.","caption":"Upper Ledges","poi_name":"","subject":"Jonathan Janicki","author":"Jonathan Janicki","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/888959","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/888959"}},{"id":"888960","ext":"png","post_id":"891608","description":"Enjoying a perfect water level on Cowanshannock.","caption":"Having a Blast","poi_name":"","subject":"Jonathan Janicki","author":"Jonathan Janicki","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/888960","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/888960"}},{"id":"888958","ext":"png","post_id":"891606","description":"The flatwater above the first rapids is about 2 miles long from the Rt 66 bridge. It is extremely beautiful though and I didn't encounter any tree portages in that area.","caption":"Upper Flatwater","poi_name":"","subject":"Jonathan Janicki","author":"Jonathan Janicki","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/888958","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/888958"}},{"id":"892864","ext":"jpg","post_id":"896629","description":null,"caption":"Cowanshannock Creek @Greendale 8.0 mi. to Route 66,  3.0 mi. to Allegheny R.","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/892864","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/892864"}},{"id":"893340","ext":"jpg","post_id":"896908","description":null,"caption":null,"poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/893340","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/893340"}},{"id":"893341","ext":"jpg","post_id":"896909","description":null,"caption":null,"poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":null,"poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/893341","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/893341"}},{"id":"kRGB4GMkXNOCtM1wnF5Gi","ext":"jpg","post_id":"61WcoNJOFuC0oDQhzdsly","description":null,"caption":null,"poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"Brandon Borish","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2022-02-25T18:54:59.000Z","updated_at":"2022-03-02T18:54:59.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/kRGB4GMkXNOCtM1wnF5Gi","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/kRGB4GMkXNOCtM1wnF5Gi"}}],"detail":{"id":"1601","permitUrl":null,"permitInfo":null,"description":"Dave Woten gives the beta:\n\nCowanshannock Creek is a small delightful run that flows into the Allegheny River in Kittanning. It is perfect for the up-and-coming creeker ready to step up from Slippery Rock Creek towards more gradient and tighter lines. Numerous tight lines to choose from, some rock banging, log boofing and even pipe dodging are all part of what makes this trip creeky.\n\nThe take-out is at the PA Fish Commission boat launch area on Cowanshannock Creek just before its confluence with the Allegheny River. You drive parallel to the run for much of the shuttle. There is a put-in area in the woods partway up the run which cuts out the flat water and a couple rapids. The long version has you putting in around route 28/66.\n\nWe hop on the creek just behind a min-mart type store. Watch for the \"no parking\" signs lining the road. Here is an obvious flat area across from the mining operation where we get a visual. If the little ripple looks like you can float downstream it is runnable. If the water is coming out of the banks into the river right grass it is definitely on the high side. The photo \"Buttermilk Falls\" at the bottom of the page was taken with the water up in the grass (very high). Compare both photos since they are the same rapid.\n\nThe Cowanshannock begins with a long stretch of flatwater, however, time passes quickly due to the fast current and enjoyable scenery. The first couple rapids are a good place to warm-up, do some eddy hopping and even a little surfing. You will soon approach a small steep slide. You can have some fun playing at the bottom. You will notice the gradient begin to pick up. Soon you will approach the steepest part of the creek beginning with Buttermilk Falls, a 2-3 foot ledge stretching from river left to 2/3 of the way across the creek. The lead-in to the falls is shallow. You can go over the drop anywhere--just stay straight. The far right line requires a more technical but easily manageable move with an ill-placed rock.\n\nIf you get out to scout Buttermilk Falls, scout the next drop while you are on the banks because it is just a few feet downstream. This drop starts center, goes sharp right, then immediately left. This happens fast with a decent amount of gradient. You can use the pillowed rock at the bottom of the drop to turn left but if you do, watch out for the hole.\n\nThe footbridge should now be very close and easily visible. Approach with caution! I have seen strainers, pipes and other \"nasties\" become lodged around the abutment and in this area of the creek. They are often right in the middle of the desired line.\n\nThough the creek continues to drop, the steepest and most difficult drops are behind you at this point. You should be able to read and run the rest of the creek as it continues to become easier. Before you know it, you will be at the take-out, ready for run number 2!\n\nThe fast run, easy shuttle, and great put-in/take-out make it a nice run on a cold snowy winter day.","averageGradient":null,"maxGradient":null,"length":"11.7","correlations":[{"reachID":"1601","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment"